WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:06.609
The original vampiric creature of Australian
mythology, the Yara-ma-yha-who, stands out

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:11.190
due to its unusual appearance and interesting
feeding habits.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.900
Small in stature, the red-skinned monster’s
other notable feature is its giant mouth.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:21.710
Though it may look silly—these beings give
a whole new meaning to the word “blood sucker.”

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:27.020
Meant to frighten children into good behavior,
it also ties the members of indigenous communities

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.820
to one another, to their environment, and
to their shared past.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.899
Oh and by the way, they drink your blood through
their fingertips.

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:37.410
And their toes.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.690
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:46.949
One of mythology’s truly unique creatures,
the Australian Yara-ma-yha-who’s unassuming,

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:51.289
almost comical appearance hides its threatening
nature.

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:57.100
Standing only about four feet tall, the red-skinned
monster has a massive, serpentine head on

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.730
a disproportionately small body.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.839
Its mouth, throat, and stomach are likewise
freakishly large.

00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:08.120
This strange being has no teeth, no weapons
to speak of.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.470
So why is it so scary?

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.750
Well, first of all, it swallows its food whole.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.600
Its mouth widens like a snake, much wider
than its body size—the better to gobble

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.000
up its prey.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.230
And what is its prey?

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.230
Us.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:26.130
Or, more accurately, it is coming for your
children.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.880
The lore surrounding the Yara-ma-yha-who functioned
primarily to frighten Aboriginal children

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.680
into obedience so that they wouldn’t wander
away from the safety of their homes and into

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:37.680
the bush.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.850
The Australian outback is a notoriously unforgiving
landscape, and you can’t blame a community

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:47.230
for developing particularly gruesome and terrifying
stories to keep their kids in line.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.840
Although it won’t turn down an unassuming
adult either.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.810
I mentioned that this monster doesn’t appear
to have any weapons…but appearances can

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.340
be deceiving, especially when we’re talking
about this diminutive fellow.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:05.280
In fact, the Yara-ma-yha-who has two grisly
weapons to employ—sucker-cup-ladened appendages

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.890
andddd…regurgitation?

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:13.260
According to one folktale, a man, tired from
hunting, fell asleep under a tree.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:19.159
Before he could even utter a sound, a Yara-ma-yha-who
leapt upon him and sucked his blood...with

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:21.299
its fingers and toes.

00:02:21.299 --> 00:02:27.300
The monster began to swallow him whole, creeping
over his body with his mouth unnaturally wide.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.560
The hunter’s mother saw what was happening,
and grabbed her husband’s spear, but she

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.370
was too far away and before she could come
close to the monster, the Yara-ma-yha-woo

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:41.080
stood and ran off to a watering hole, his
new victim in his stomach.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.709
While telling the tale to little children
in her community, the mother lamented the

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:48.159
Yara-ma-yha-who must have vomited him back
up.

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:52.849
A smart man, she instructed, would have played
dead until dark when the monster was asleep

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:54.359
and it was safer to flee.

00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:59.749
Her son, she believes, tried to escape, but
the monster caught him--only to swallow him

00:02:59.749 --> 00:03:01.099
up again.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:02.230
And again.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.879
Until he became like the monster himself.

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:07.590
He had turned into a Yara-ma-yah-who.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:12.749
Living among the thick, leafy foliage of wild
fig trees, Yara-ma-yha-who will pounce on

00:03:12.749 --> 00:03:17.760
its prey -- men, women, and children alike
-- as they walk below, or are foolish enough

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.999
to seek respite under the trees’ branches.

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:25.440
Fear of this creature was so great that people
would go out of their way to look for shelter

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.840
in caves or rocky overhangs rather than sit
under a tree.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.360
The Yara-ma-yha-who’s fingers and toes each
end in a cup-shaped sucker like those of an

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.299
octopus, but these suckers drain their victim’s
blood.

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:45.000
And it gets worse: Yara-ma-yha-who drains
just enough blood from its victims to incapacitate

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.000
them.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.730
Then, like a cat with a mouse, the games begin.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:54.359
Once the victim is unconscious, the Yara-ma-yha-who
crawls toward it and swallows the body whole,

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:55.370
headfirst.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.840
This apparently takes considerable energy,
requiring the monster to take a long drink

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.299
of water and then curl up for a nap—the
victim still alive in its belly.

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:10.159
Upon waking, the Yara-ma-yha-who vomits up
its (presumably terrified) meal.

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:12.019
And it gets weirder.

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:16.700
Only once the creature falls asleep again
(it clearly has a thing for naps), its victim

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.060
can run away.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.539
And if the Yara-ma-yha-who wakes, the victim
may be safe since the monster cannot run very

00:04:22.539 --> 00:04:26.569
fast, its gate resembling that of a cockatoo.

00:04:26.569 --> 00:04:27.569
BUT

00:04:27.569 --> 00:04:29.690
You knew where was going to be a “But,”
didn’t you?

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:33.120
It’s not just the loss of blood that the
human prey must fear.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.330
If a person is caught by the Yara-ma-yha-who
more than once, they will become shorter and

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:44.449
shorter each time it regurgitates them until
they become as small as the monster itself.

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:49.070
Slowly their skin will turn smooth and red,
and they will lose all of the hair on their

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.070
body.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.630
Eventually, they become a Yara-ma-yha-who.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.449
That’s the creature.

00:04:54.449 --> 00:04:59.479
Hauntingly creepy and yet surprisingly practical
from a community organization standpoint,

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:00.479
right?

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:05.139
But I mentioned earlier that this monster’s
story is deeper than just a warning to kids

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:08.340
about the dangers of the wilderness that surrounded
them.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:13.770
Like many of the monsters I explore, Yara-ma-yha-who’s
story is intrinsically tied both to the cultural

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:17.669
identity and to the history of the people
who mythologized it.

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:22.370
The pervasive mythology of the indigenous
peoples of Australia reflects their deep ties

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:27.060
to the land and the environment, as well as
the far-reaching effects of Colonialism on

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.949
these cultures.

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:33.509
Aboriginal communities recognize three important
categories outside of humans and animals in

00:05:33.509 --> 00:05:39.410
their mythos: Ancestral Creators, ghosts or
human spirits, and spirit beings.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:44.540
The Ancestral Creators occupied the land during
what the Aboriginals call Dreamtime, the beginning

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:45.669
of time.

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:50.330
They shaped the entire world from sky to sea
and everything in-between.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:54.699
They dictated the traditions and customs of
social and religious practice.

00:05:54.699 --> 00:05:59.630
For Aboriginals, humans exist because of these
Ancestor Beings.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:03.599
Ghosts or human spirits are understood to
be the spiritual remains of the living.

00:06:03.599 --> 00:06:06.289
Finally, there are the spirit beings.

00:06:06.289 --> 00:06:11.639
Unlike the Ancestral Creators, who are beings
from a time long ago, spirit beings exist

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:16.080
in the here-and-now, living on the land alongside
humans.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.620
Aboriginal sacred beliefs see such creatures
—including the Yara-ma-yha-who—as “co-residents”

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.849
of a shared landscape.

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:27.139
The spirit beings are part of the physical
environment in that they contribute in ways

00:06:27.139 --> 00:06:30.169
that may directly influence the lives of people.

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:34.830
Among these spirit beings is a whole category
of “little men” or “spirit men,” a

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.830
group to which the Yara-ma-yha-who belong.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.540
Anthropologist Philip A. Clarke explored this
classification deeply.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:46.160
According to him, these small beings with
human-like characteristics, unusually colored

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.320
skin (most commonly red), and a mischievous
nature are often connected to a particular

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.560
environment or locale.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:57.909
These “spirit men” [kintji or kindi] are
predominant in myths among South-eastern Australian

00:06:57.909 --> 00:06:59.139
Aboriginals.

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:04.530
That being said, there is striking differentiation
and variation in their appearance and habits

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:08.729
across communities and even within a single
community.

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:13.169
Moreover, there are other “little men”
who appear throughout Australian lore.

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:18.110
In Western Australia, the Yara-ma-yha-who’s
counterpart looks more like an orangutan or

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.680
a monkey and has a hairy—but still red—body

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:25.719
In fact, one thing almost all the stories
have in common, is the unusual coloring of

00:07:25.719 --> 00:07:26.719
the little men.

00:07:26.719 --> 00:07:32.300
They could be yellow, green, grey, white,
or red like our friend the Yara-ma-yha-who.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:37.000
The broader group of these little men generally
live on high ground whether it be on the tops

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.889
of cliffs, hills, or sand dunes.

00:07:39.889 --> 00:07:44.389
Most active at dawn and dusk, they are spotted
either in pairs or alone.

00:07:44.389 --> 00:07:48.139
Hunters would leave out offerings from their
kills to appease these beings.

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:53.650
Of course, there are ways in which Yara-ma-yha-who
tales differ from those of these other “spirit

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:54.650
men.”

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.389
They are not reported in pairs, and they do
most of their hunting during the day.

00:07:58.389 --> 00:08:03.789
They are truly unique, however, in that they
alone threaten to make you one of their kind.

00:08:03.789 --> 00:08:08.719
Before the British forcibly settled on the
continent, over 500 Indigenous groups occupied

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:09.919
the land.

00:08:09.919 --> 00:08:14.749
Many of their myths and stories were misinterpreted
by the Europeans, and creatures from Aboriginal

00:08:14.749 --> 00:08:20.389
mythology were regarded as real foreign species
by European colonizers—beings that they

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:26.410
had yet to see in the wild but were certain
existed, like the bunyip and even, to a degree,

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.289
the Yara-ma-yha-who.

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:33.690
Early 20th-century anthropologist and archeologist
Norman B. Tindale believed that accounts of

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:38.289
the “spirit men” or “little folk”
were actually the diluted oral history of

00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:43.700
a race of pygmy people believed to have arrived
in Australia more than 30,000 years ago.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:48.769
The increasing exchange of cultural practices
and folklore in post-colonial Australia meant

00:08:48.769 --> 00:08:54.889
that indigenous myths were forever altered—including
the mythology describing its “monsters.”

00:08:54.889 --> 00:08:59.990
This helps explain, for example, why the Yara-ma-yha-who
is more often classified as a “vampire”

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:04.950
species in modern times, rather than as the
“spirit being” it originally was understood

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:05.950
to be.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:11.370
It also helps us understand why we see such
variation in the monsters’ traits, across

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:13.380
cultures and throughout modern history.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:18.740
Still, reported sightings of various diminutive
humanoid beings in Australia continued well

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:24.410
into the 1980s, and folklore of the “spirit
men” continues to be shared among members

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:26.510
of indigenous communities today.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:31.790
The continuation of the oral traditions of
spirit beings by the Aboriginal people serves

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:36.720
as a connection to their regional communities
and as part of a larger cultural identity

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.170
in post-colonial Australia.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:54.900
The Yara-ma-yha-who might be a strange body—but
it is nonetheless a body of knowledge.

